  "tibetan_transliteration": "",
  "comments": "[GEHU2V2G]: 121 discusses the identity of the author named \"N\u0101g\u0101rjuna\" - possibly not by the same N\u0101g\u0101rjuna who authored the M\u016blamadhyamakak\u0101rik\u0101, etc. - noting that there is no evidence that the work was known to Indian scholars before the 10th century.\r\n\r\nThirteen translations are reported to have existed ([GEHU2V2G]: 127).\r\nThe canonical Tibetan translation is the revision, by Kanakavarma and Pa tshab Nyi ma grags, of the original translation from Sanskrit by  Gun\u0323a\u0304kara and Rab zhi shes gnyen ([WCM64QPA]: 183).\r\nA different translation can be extracted from the Tibetan commentary (ID:8473), which may be influenced or derived from the Tibetan translation of gTsang nag pa (ID:3839). Another version was preserved in the Jo bo'i chos chung rgya rtsa (see [WCM64QPA], which translates the verses based on the version in (ID:8473)).\r\n\r\nOn the Chinese and Tibetan translations, see [GEHU2V2G]: 128-132.",
